sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: orchestrator 2026-11-08: keeping approved work is a precondition of the cancel, not a step of it. 2026-11-15: the task VMs and their release are removed (docs/reference/old-paths.md); a cancel starts no run. 2026-11-16: the place release workflows are deleted; `operation-task-cancel` keeps its inputs and its end check, and calls no release.
  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-13: a task copy ends through its Instance destroy. Orchestrator 2026-11-14: work that only the copy holds, or a copy that cannot be read, refuses the cancel unless the caller discards it; the cancel is recorded under the group lock, in one transaction with the destroy's admission, after the destroy's reads. A commit a publish pushed counts as on origin. 2026-11-16 (group 5615): published means content on the default branch, not commit SHAs: the copy's content read, made by the `base` phase that task:sync runs, decides for committed work, so a squash-merged or hand-carried copy cancels without discard.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: task:cancel
  %% kind: tasks.cancel
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant GH as GitHub<br/>repository
  Note over GW: Check before any change<br/>cancellable
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the group is cancellable; otherwise `tasks.not_cancellable`
  %%> - every refusal here and in the next step comes before the cancel stops running work, so a refused cancel changes nothing
  GW->>GH: Compare the task branch<br/>approved...task-42
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - resolves the group's latest approved commit: the latest commit Orbit reviewed (ADR 0203), or else the commit of the latest approval
  %%> - reads origin's task branch head and compares it with that commit, with a `contents: read` token
  %%> - only when GitHub answers that the branch does not exist (404) and the group's pull request merged does that pull request's head stand in; the pull request is read with a `pull_requests: read` token
  %%> - skipped when the group has no approval, or holds no workspace
  %%> - for a task copy, content on the default branch counts too: the copy's latest content read (`work`, of a `task:sync`) is of the copy's current head, holds the approved commit in its history and found every committed path on the default branch
  GH-->>GW: Branch reaches it
  %%> Result:
  %%> - otherwise the cancel refuses with HTTP 409 `tasks.approved_work_unpublished`, names the commit and the branch, and admits nothing: run `task:publish` first
  %%> - every other outcome refuses the same way: a GitHub read that fails, a branch that is gone without a merged pull request
  alt the task owns a task copy
    Note over GW: Read the copy's workspace<br/>one bounded read
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - one SSH call to the copy's Node, bounded at 4 s, reads what the copy holds beyond its seed: commits that origin lacks, uncommitted, untracked and stashed files
    %%> - a copy never fetches, so the commits that the group's publishes pushed are named to the read as on origin, with their history
    %%> - a stopped or unreachable copy reads `unknown` with its reason
    %%> - committed work counts by content, not by commit id: when the copy's latest content read (`work` of a `task:sync`, docs/reference/tasks.md#published-work-of-a-copy) is of the head this read sees and found every committed path on the default branch, the checked-out branch's own commits count as published, as after a squash merge or a hand-carry; a commit that only another local branch holds still counts
    %%> Result:
    %%> - unless the caller discards the work, a copy that holds work (`dirty`: unpublished commits, uncommitted or untracked files, stashes) refuses the cancel with HTTP 409 `tasks.copy_unpublished_work`; its details carry the counts, the head, the commits the read counted and the content verdict `work`: `on_default`, `work_not_on_default`, or `work_unread` when no content read is of this head (run `task:sync`, then cancel again)
    %%> - unless the caller discards it, a copy that cannot be read (`unknown`) refuses with HTTP 409 `tasks.copy_workspace_unknown`, naming the reason
    %%> - a `clean` copy goes on; both refusals come before the cancel stops running work, so they change nothing
    Note over GW: Record, then admit destroy<br/>one transaction · group lock
    %%> Requirements:
    %%> - no Operation holds the copy, such as its create or an earlier destroy; otherwise HTTP 409 `operation.target_busy`, naming it
    %%> - after the group's running work was asked to stop, no Operation still holds the group, such as a timed-out one whose run nothing has proven ended; otherwise HTTP 409 `operation.target_busy`, naming it, and no destroy is admitted
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the destroy's checks and the Schedule stop come first, outside any transaction: its replication slot check over SSH, then the stop of the copy's Schedules (`schedule.destroy` Operations and their removal in Swamp)
    %%> - then, in the destroy's one transaction, records the cancel under the group's lock key first and admits the normal `instance.destroy` of the copy with force
    %%> - an Operation that takes the group lock first makes the record refuse with HTTP 409 `operation.target_busy`; the transaction rolls back, so a refused cancel never admits the destroy; the copy's Schedules stay stopped
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the cancel is recorded `running` without the group lock; its result names the destroy Operation and holds the workspace read
    %%> - the destroy runs as every Instance destroy does; the cancel ends when it ends and has no budget of its own: the destroy's budget bounds both
    %%> - when the destroy succeeds, a task that still runs and its open subtasks are `cancelled`, the task lets go of the copy, and the cancel succeeds; a `failed` task stays `failed`
    %%> - when it fails or its budget expires, the cancel fails with the destroy's error code, such as `instance.removal_incomplete` or `operation.timed_out`; the task runs on and keeps the copy; the next cancel admits the destroy again; a stopped or half-removed copy may read `unknown`, so that cancel may need the discard
  else no task copy and no workspace
    Note over GW: End the task at admission<br/>R1: no run
    %%> When:
    %%> - the task holds no workspace, such as a `todo` task or one whose task VM was removed; its open Operations are cancelled first
    %%> - a task that still holds a workspace that is no task copy (a task VM workspace) refuses with HTTP 409 `tasks.workspace_ownership_refused`: nothing can release it any more
    %%> Result:
    %%> - in the admission transaction, the task and its open subtasks are `cancelled` and the Operation is recorded as succeeded; no Swamp run starts (R1)
    %%> - the HTTP 202 already carries the succeeded Operation, so `task:show` reads `cancelled` from then on
  end
